From: MacGameVideoFix <https://github.com/MathiasKowoll/MacGameVideoFix>
Subject: [PATCH] winebus.sys: a stronger rumble for a DualSense on Bluetooth, and an intensity setting

WHERE THIS CAME FROM

MacGameVideoFix's own series, mgvf-NNNN, written on 2026-09-09, the eighth of
ours and the seventh in the controller-bus set. Unlike mgvf-0007 and mgvf-0008
it is NOT a follow-up to mgvf-0005: it acts on the pad's own Bluetooth output
report, so it works for a client that was never lied to as well as for one that
was, and its main case is the first of those. Nothing in it is specific to this
project.

THIS IS A PREFERENCE, NOT A REPAIR

Everything the set does elsewhere repairs something measured to be wrong. This
changes what a game asked for into something the person holding the pad likes
better, which is a different kind of claim, and it is why both values below are
OFF by default: a build installed with nothing under the pad's key sends every
packet exactly as the client wrote it, byte for byte and CRC included.

WHAT THE GAME ACTUALLY ASKS FOR

steam-hid-game-145815.log, a two-hour Bluetooth session of an Unreal title that
drives the pad through Unreal's WinDualShock, on 2026-09-08. The pad is
presented truthfully -- no emulation, no mgvf-0005 -- and the title writes the
pad's own report itself:

    /usr/bin/grep -c "write output report id 49 length 78"  ->  6493

6493 output reports, every one of them a 78-byte 0x31. 335 of those carry a
non-zero motor byte, and they reach the top of the scale: 36 packets at right
255 with the left motor at rest, 18 with both motors at 255, and the rest spread
below -- 17 at 89/0, 12 at 153/0, 11 at 38/25, 10 at 38/0, 8 at 76/76.

ALL 335 CARRY THE SAME MODE. First flag byte 0x02, second flag byte 0x00, and
byte 38 of the common block 0x04. Not most of them: all of them. So the game is
not asking for a weak effect and there is no gain to be found by looking harder
at what it sends -- at 255/255 it is asking for everything there is. It is
asking for full strength on one particular path.

THE TWO PATHS, AND WHERE THEIR NAMES COME FROM

Not from memory. SDL 2.30.12's src/joystick/hidapi/SDL_hidapi_ps5.c, read from
the copy in this build directory, lays out this exact block field for field --
ucEnableBits1 at 0, ucRumbleRight at 2, ucRumbleLeft at 3, ucEnableBits3 at 38
-- and its rumble path chooses between two ways of driving the motors by the
pad's firmware version:

    firmware < 2.24     ucEnableBits1 |= 0x01   /* Enable rumble emulation */
                        and BOTH motor bytes are halved, with the comment
                        "Shift to reduce effective rumble strength to match
                        Xbox controllers"

    firmware >= 2.24    ucEnableBits3 |= 0x04   /* Enable improved rumble
                        emulation on 2.24 firmware and newer */
                        and the motor bytes are sent as they are

    both branches       ucEnableBits1 |= 0x02   /* Disable audio haptics */

The mode in the log is the second of those, bit for bit: 0x02 in the first flag
byte, 0x04 in byte 38. The first is what this patch can put in its place.

THE EVIDENCE FOR PREFERRING THE OTHER ONE, AND HOW SOFT IT IS

The owner compared the two directly on the pad with a six-pulse ladder --
dsrumble3.c, which sends this exact packet through IOKit at 255 and at 127 in
three modes: legacy (flag0 0x01|0x02), the haptic path (flag0 0x02, byte 38
0x04), and both bits at once -- and reported that LEGACY COMPATIBLE VIBRATION
AT 255 FEELS CLEARLY STRONGER than the haptic mode at 255, and that the haptic
mode at 127 is what the game feels like.

THAT PERCEPTION IS THE EVIDENCE FOR THIS PATCH. It is a preference, measured on
one person's hand, not a physical measurement of anything: no accelerometer, no
current draw, no repetition, nothing blind. It is stated here as what it is
because it is the whole of the case, and it is why the rewrite has to be asked
for.

THE CHANGE

Two REG_DWORD values under the key mgvf-0005's UsbEmulation, mgvf-0006's
SeizeDevice and mgvf-0008's ForwardFeatureWrites already live under, named
after the pad's real ids in lower-case hex with a slash:

    HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\winebus\Devices\<vid>/<pid>

        VibrationMode  REG_DWORD  absent or 0 (default): every packet goes out
                                  exactly as the client wrote it.
                                  1: where the client selected the haptic path,
                                  the legacy compatible motors are selected
                                  instead.

        VibrationGain  REG_DWORD  a percentage over the two motor bytes. Absent
                                  or 100: untouched. It saturates at 255, so a
                                  game that asks for 89 can be made to ask for
                                  more and one already asking for 255 cannot go
                                  higher. Clamped to 1000, and 0 reads as
                                  absent.

Scoped to a hidraw DualSense (054c:0ce6, 054c:0df2) that arrived over
Bluetooth, which is where the corpus and the ladder both are. A pad on a cable,
a DualShock 4 and every other device are untouched, and both values are read
once, as the device arrives, before mgvf-0005 has a chance to replace the pid
in the desc -- mgvf-0008's reason for reading where it reads, and the same one.

The clamp is 1000 rather than something larger because 1000% is already enough
to saturate any motor value of 26 or more, and a larger ceiling would only make
an unintended overflow easier to write by hand. It is a chosen limit, not a
measured one; the corpus itself has motor values all the way down to 1.
VibrationGain 0 is read as absence rather than as silence, so that a value
written as 0 and a key with no value cannot mean two different things -- the
reading mgvf-0005 gives UsbEmulation. A gain of 1 is how to make the pad nearly
silent.

WHICH BIT IS CLEARED, WHICH IS SET, AND WHY EACH

    SET flag0 bit 0x01, the legacy compatible motors. It is the bit SDL sets
    when it wants the motors driven the older way, and the bit the ladder's
    mode A set for the pulse that was preferred. Set rather than assigned, so
    the rest of the byte survives.

    CLEAR byte 38 bit 0x04, the haptic path's own motor emulation. It is the
    bit SDL sets for the other branch and the only bit of that byte the 335
    measured packets carry -- their byte 38 is exactly 0x04, nothing else.
    Leaving it set while setting 0x01 would ask the pad for both at once, which
    is the ladder's mode C: a third thing, and not the one that was preferred.

    LEAVE flag0 bit 0x02 alone. SDL sets it on BOTH branches, so it is not the
    choice between them; all three of the ladder's modes carried it; and what
    its own comment says it does -- "Disable audio haptics" -- is not this
    patch's business.

    LEAVE EVERY OTHER BYTE alone, the second flag byte included, for the reason
    mgvf-0007 leaves it alone: nothing measured says what its other bits mean.

    DO NOT HALVE THE MOTORS. SDL does, on the legacy branch, to match an Xbox
    pad. Doing the same here would undo exactly what the ladder found: it sent
    the SAME value on both paths and the legacy one felt stronger.
    VibrationGain is the separate and explicit way to change the number.

BOTH ROUTES, BECAUSE THE MEASURED ONE IS NOT THE EMULATED ONE

The title above writes report 0x31 itself, with no emulation involved, so a
hook that lived only in mgvf-0005's translation would never see a single packet
this patch was written for. An output report 0x31 arriving for such a pad is
rewritten on the raw route too, in a COPY -- the client's buffer is not ours to
change -- and in the emulation path the rewrite happens after the translation
and after mgvf-0007's masking, so that what is rewritten is the packet as it
would otherwise have gone out.

Either way THE CRC IS COMPUTED AGAIN, over the bytes actually being sent. That
is not tidiness: the pad checks it and drops a 0x31 whose four trailing bytes
do not cover the report, so rewriting a byte without re-signing would take the
game's rumble away entirely rather than change it.

Nothing is written when nothing is asked for, and the caller can tell: the
rewrite returns 0 for a report the options have nothing to say about, and the
driver then forwards the client's own buffer, unread and uncopied.

THE TRACE

One line when a packet is rewritten, carrying both halves of everything it
touched -- flag0 before and after, byte 38 before and after, both motors before
and after -- because the next trace has to show plainly what was done and what
was left. TRACE and not WARN, as mgvf-0007's line is: with the option on this
fires on every packet that asks for a motor, hundreds of times in a session,
and it is what the option is for rather than something going wrong.

THE HOST TEST

usb_emu_test.c beside the descriptors in the build directory, case (o), against
four packets pulled out of that log by extract-steam-packets.py: the haptic-mode
packets at 255/0, at 38/25 and at 255/255, and the all-zero one the title writes
when it wants nothing. It checks that the mode rewrite moves the two bits and no
others and leaves the motors alone; that a gain of 200 takes 38/25 to 76/50 and
that 255 saturates and writes nothing at all; that a gain of 100000 is clamped;
that both values absent -- and a gain of 100, which means the same -- leave every
one of the four byte-identical, the title's own CRC included; and that a packet
with no motor and no mode is left alone even with both options on. The expected
bytes are written out in the test from the byte offsets by hand, not by calling
the code under test.

NOT COVERED

WHY THE TWO PATHS FEEL DIFFERENT is not established. Whether the pad drives
different actuators, or the same ones differently, or whether this pad's
firmware simply implements one of them better than the other, is unknown, and
nothing here reads the pad's firmware version -- so which path Sony intended for
this pad is unknown too. What is established is that the game asks for one of
them, at full scale, and that a person preferred the other.

THAT THE VIBRATION IS ACTUALLY STRONGER is a perception and stays one in this
header wherever it appears. Two engine builds compared by hand is also how the
owner noticed that the set without mgvf-0006 vibrated more weakly than the set
with it; that is the same kind of evidence and is recorded the same way.

NO TITLE HAS BEEN PLAYED WITH THE REWRITE ON. What is measured is the ladder,
by hand, and the corpus the mode was read out of. The first use belongs in a
+hid trace, where the line above will say what was changed.

FIVE OF THE 6493 PACKETS ARE NOT LIKE THE OTHERS and are recorded here without
an explanation: they are 78 bytes of 31 02 00 and then zeros, with no CRC that
checks out and no mode bits at all. This patch does not touch them -- they
select no way to vibrate -- but what wrote them, and what the pad did with
them, has not been looked into.

